19:T67f,## The problem: a matched set

Imagine a cross-platform UI toolkit. On Windows you want Windows widgets; on macOS you want Mac widgets. A screen is built from many pieces, a button, a checkbox, a scrollbar, and they all have to come from the **same family**. A Mac-style button next to a Windows-style checkbox looks broken.

The danger is **mixing**: somewhere deep in the code, someone news up a `WinButton` while everything else is Mac. You need a way to make that mistake impossible.

## The Abstract Factory

An **abstract factory** is an interface that declares **one create-method per product** in the family:

```
interface GUIFactory {
    Button   makeButton();
    Checkbox makeCheckbox();
}
```

Each **concrete factory** builds one consistent family, `WinFactory` returns all-Windows widgets, `MacFactory` returns all-Mac widgets. The client codes against the `GUIFactory`, `Button`, and `Checkbox` **interfaces**, picks a concrete factory **once** at startup, and from then on every product it receives is guaranteed to match.

## How it differs from Factory Method

**Factory Method** creates **one** product through a single overridable method. **Abstract Factory** creates a whole **family of related products** through a set of methods grouped behind one factory object — so the pieces are guaranteed to be compatible with each other.

## When to use it

- Your system needs to work with **multiple families** of related products (themes, platforms, drivers).
- You must **guarantee that products from one family are used together** and never mixed.
- You want to swap the entire family by changing **one** line, which factory you instantiate.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-creational","activeLesson":"abstract-factory"}],["$","$L18","abstract-factory",{"lesson":{"id":"abstract-factory","patternId":"lld-creational","title":"Abstract Factory","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Advanced","statement":"Create whole FAMILIES of related objects (a Button + a Checkbox that match) through one factory, guaranteeing the pieces always belong together.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"One factory, a whole family","code":["interface GUIFactory {","    Button   makeButton();    // one method per product","    Checkbox makeCheckbox();  //   in the family","}","class WinFactory implements GUIFactory { ... }  // all-Windows","class MacFactory implements GUIFactory { ... }  // all-Mac","","GUIFactory f = onMac ? new MacFactory() : new WinFactory();","Button   b = f.makeButton();","Checkbox k = f.makeCheckbox();   // guaranteed to match b"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"GUIFactory declares makeButton() and makeCheckbox(), one create-method per product in the family.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"fac","name":"GUIFactory","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ makeButton()","+ makeCheckbox()"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"win","name":"WinFactory","x":0,"y":1.5},{"id":"mac","name":"MacFactory","x":1,"y":1.5},{"id":"btn","name":"Button","stereotype":"interface","x":2.4,"y":0.45,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"chk","name":"Checkbox","stereotype":"interface","x":2.4,"y":1.5,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"r1","from":"win","to":"fac","kind":"implement"},{"id":"r2","from":"mac","to":"fac","kind":"implement"},{"id":"r3","from":"fac","to":"btn","kind":"depend"},{"id":"r4","from":"fac","to":"chk","kind":"depend"}],"shownClassIds":["fac"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["fac"]},"vars":[["factory","o